<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Roberto de Nobili</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1577–1656</span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Roberto de Nobili was an Italian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> missionary who worked at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madurai">Madurai</a> from 1606. He adopted the dress, diet and customs of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> sannyasi, learned Tamil and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a>, and presented <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> in Indian terms to win converts from the high <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">castes</a>. His methods caused a long controversy within the Church but were approved by Pope Gregory XV in 1623.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/roberto-de-nobili.jpg" alt="Roberto de Nobili" width="320" height="455" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Grentidez · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Roberto_de_Nobili_(1577-1656),_gravure.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
